    "verifiedAt": "2026-11-23",
    "description": "In \"Courting Komachi,\" part of the series \"Famous Scenes from Japanese Puppet Plays,\" artist Okumura Masanobu captures a poignant moment from a Kabuki drama inspired by the legendary ninth-century poet Ono no Komachi. The scene depicts the ghost of a suitor who, unable to fulfill his promise to visit Komachi for 100 consecutive days due to a storm, returns to her in the dead of night. This woodblock print, created during the Edo period (1705-6), is a striking example of Japanese art, with only two museums, The Art Institute and the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston, housing a complete set of this series.",
    "descriptionTranslations": {
      "ja": "奥村政信の「恋する小町」は、「日本の人形劇名場面」シリーズの一部で、9世紀の詩人・小野小町に基づく歌舞伎の場面を描いています。100日間連続で訪れることを約束した求婚者が嵐に遭い、最終日には現れず、彼の霊が夜中に小町を訪れる様子が表現されています。この版画は江戸時代（1705-6）の作品で、アート・インスティチュートとボストン美術館の2館のみがこのシリーズの完全なセットを所蔵しています。"
    },
